  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two kinds of behavior at a domain wall in ferroelastic or ferroelectric materials. On the left, a single primary order parameter, labeled Q, changes smoothly across the wall, passing through zero at the center. This produces the familiar strain distortion shown on the upper right: the lattice tilts in opposite ways on the two sides, with a narrow transition region between them.\n\nThe lower half adds a second quantity, polarization P, that appears only near the wall as an “emerging order parameter.” While Q still varies smoothly across the boundary, P forms a sharp localized peak centered at the wall, indicating that the interface itself can become polar even if the surrounding domains are not.\n\nThe three right-hand lower panels illustrate different possible polarization textures associated with this wall-confined state. One is Ising-like, where polarization switches mainly by reversing through the wall. Another is Bloch-like, where the polarization rotates within the wall, forming a curling pattern. The third is Néel-like, where polarization rotates in a different plane and appears to point more directly across the wall. Overall, the figure emphasizes that domain walls can host distinct, localized polar states and different switching structures.",
